          "page": 6,
          "section": "EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURE",
          "quote": "All specimens were initially solution annealed at 4000' F (2477 K) for 30 minutes in an induction furnace with a tungsten susceptor. The annealing was performed in a pressure of less than torr. Large carbon losses (40 to 60 percent) occurred during the 4000' F (2477 K) vacuum anneal. Therefore, a small amount of molybdenum carbide (Mo2C) powder was thus placed in the crucible with the specimens in order to minimize decarburization. Maximum losses of 10 percent were found using this technique. After heat treatment, the specimens were quenched by turning off the power and introducing helium gas into the furnace.",
